The human hippocampus is a crucial component of the brain's medial temporal lobe, primarily responsible for forming new memories and regulating emotions. Due to its significance in memory processes and its vulnerability to epilepsy and Alzheimer's disease, researchers have been exploring ways to precisely map its sub-regions. A innovative approach involves using numerous convolutional neural networks to analyze hippocampal sub-regions and develop a labeling system that can detect seizure and Alzheimer's related changes. By developing an automatic segmentation method with high accuracy and robustness, researchers can better understand hippocampal sub-regions, ultimately leading to breakthroughs in detecting epilepsy and Alzheimer's and improving our understanding of these conditions. A related demo can be found at: https://hippocampus.github.io/. Our work has been accepted for presentation at the CVPR 2021 Grand Challenge in Neurology, a competition organized by the Medical Computer Vision Grand Challenge. The related data and code can be found at: https://github.com/. The corresponding video can be found at: https://youtu.be/. Our findings have been presented at the KORESPEL 2021 conference. 

